<p class="sst-lead">Each measurement gives a <em>distribution</em> of separable features &mdash; undulations
\(\bm{t}\) and scales \(\bm{\ell}\). We compare two distributions with the <strong>Maximum Mean
Discrepancy</strong>.</p>

<p>\[ \mathrm{MMD}[\mathcal{F},\hat{\rho},\check{\rho}] = \sup_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\big|\,\mathbb{E}_{\hat{\bm{\theta}}\sim\hat{\rho}}[f(\hat{\bm{\theta}})] - \mathbb{E}_{\check{\bm{\theta}}\sim\check{\rho}}[f(\check{\bm{\theta}})]\,\big|. \]</p>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>The maximizing \(f\) is the <strong>witness function</strong> \(f^*\) &mdash; it doesn't just say
  <em>whether</em> two shapes differ, it shows <em>where</em> in feature space they differ.</p>
  <p class="sst-note">Slide the clouds apart: at coincidence the finite-sample MMD is small <em>but not zero</em>,
  so the verdict is set by a <strong>permutation null</strong>, not a fixed cutoff &mdash; we reject only when the
  observed MMD beats the null at \(\alpha=0.05\).</p>
</div>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>Run it on undulation \(\bm{t}\) and on scale \(\bm{\ell}\) separately, and the product test
  \(\mathrm{pMMD}[\mathcal{H}\otimes\mathcal{Q}]\) yields a <em>logical</em> verdict: same, or different because of
  scale, or different because of undulation.</p>
  <p class="sst-note">Honesty check: the decision is only as trustworthy as its calibration. The threshold must
  come from the permutation null (it controls false rejections exactly, in any dimension), and power still depends
  on kernel bandwidth and on having enough shapes relative to the feature dimension \(r\).</p>
</div>
